// Homework 1: Introduction to R (Oct 30), Moodle quiz in GIFT format (UTF-8).
// Import: Question bank -> Import -> GIFT format. All answers were checked in R.
// Code is in [html] format, so < > & are written as &lt; &gt; &amp;
// and the GIFT special characters ~ = # { } : are escaped with a backslash.

$CATEGORY: Homework 1 - Introduction to R

::Data for this quiz::[html]<p>Run this code in RStudio, then answer the questions. Round as asked.</p>
<pre>gene_id    &lt;- c("gene01", "gene02", "gene03", "gene04", "gene05", "gene06", "gene07", "gene08")
length_bp  &lt;- c(1250, 830, 2410, 1720, 960, 3050, 1480, 640)
gc_content &lt;- c(0.52, 0.47, 0.61, NA, 0.44, 0.58, 0.50, 0.39)
chrom      &lt;- c("chr1", "chr2", "chr1", "chr3", "chr2", "chr1", "chr3", "chr2")
expressed  &lt;- c(TRUE, FALSE, TRUE, TRUE, FALSE, TRUE, TRUE, FALSE)
genes &lt;- data.frame(gene_id, length_bp, gc_content, chrom, expressed)</pre>

::Q1 Select a cell::[html]<p>Using <code>[row, column]</code>, get the length (in bp) of the 5th gene in <code>genes</code>. What is it?</p>{#
=960:0
=%0%-100000000..100000000#Hint\: <code>length_bp</code> is the 2nd column; the row number goes first inside <code>[ ]</code>.
}

::Q2 Filter rows::[html]<p>Which code returns the IDs of the genes longer than 1500 bp?</p>{
=<code>genes$gene_id[genes$length_bp &gt; 1500]</code>
~<code>genes[genes$length_bp &gt; 1500]</code>#Hint\: without a comma, <code>[ ]</code> on a data frame selects columns, not rows.
~<code>genes$gene_id[genes$length_bp &gt; 1500, ]</code>#Hint\: <code>genes$gene_id</code> is a vector; a vector has only one dimension.
~<code>genes$gene_id(genes$length_bp &gt; 1500)</code>#Hint\: round brackets call a function; selecting uses square brackets.
}

::Q3 Two conditions::[html]<p>How many genes are on chr1 <b>and</b> expressed?</p>{#
=3:0
=%0%-100000000..100000000#Hint\: combine two conditions with <code>&amp;</code>; <code>sum()</code> of a logical vector counts the TRUE values.
}

::Q4 Several values::[html]<p>Using <code>%in%</code>, count the genes that are on chr2 or chr3. How many are there?</p>{#
=5:0
=%0%-100000000..100000000#Hint\: Use <code>%in%</code> with a vector of the two chromosome names, then count the TRUE values.
}

::Q5 Missing value::[html]<p>Why does <code>mean(genes$gc_content)</code> return <code>NA</code>?</p>{
=One value is missing (NA), and <code>mean()</code> does not remove NA by default
~<code>gc_content</code> is a character vector#Hint\: check it with <code>class(genes$gc_content)</code>.
~The data frame has no column <code>gc_content</code>#Hint\: check the columns with <code>str(genes)</code>.
~Proportions cannot be averaged#Hint\: look at the values themselves; is one of them special?
}

::Q6 Mean GC content::[html]<p>Calculate the mean GC content, ignoring the missing value. Round to 3 decimals.</p>{#
=0.501:0.001
=%0%-100000000..100000000#Hint\: <code>mean()</code> has an argument that removes NA; see <code>?mean</code>.
}

::Q7 Your own function::[html]<p>Write a function <code>to_percent()</code> that multiplies a vector by 100. What is the 3rd value of <code>to_percent(genes$gc_content)</code>?</p>{#
=61:0.01
=%0%-100000000..100000000#Hint\: <code>to_percent &lt;- function(x) \{ ... \}</code>; then take the 3rd element of the result with <code>[ ]</code>.
}

::Q8 Mean of a subset::[html]<p>What is the mean length (in bp) of the <b>expressed</b> genes?</p>{#
=1982:0
=%0%-100000000..100000000#Hint\: select <code>length_bp</code> with the logical column <code>expressed</code> as a filter, then take the mean.
}
